implementationPlan.description:
Edit packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/qaReject.ts. This is the only runtime file; docs/MCP_SERVER.md is held by another row and is not edited here.

Changes:
- Add `const MAX_FAILED_DOD_ITEM_LENGTH = 1000;` above VALID_ISSUE_TYPES, with a one-line comment: an entry names a DoD item, which is stored at 1000 chars at most (taskStore's sanitizeStringArray(definitionOfDone, 50, 1000)).
- Replace the count-only check at :86-89 with one validation block, still before the issues validation and before any lookup or write:
  - when failedDodItems is not null or undefined, a non-array is refused with invalidInput('failedDodItems', 'must be an array of strings');
  - the existing 'too many items (max 20)' refusal stays, with the same text;
  - each entry that is not a string is refused with invalidInput(`failedDodItems[${i}]`, 'must be a string');
  - each entry whose trimmed length exceeds the bound is refused with invalidInput(`failedDodItems[${i}]`, `too long (<n> chars). Maximum 1000 characters.`). Measure the trimmed length, because that is what gets stored.
  - Block comment: refused, not truncated, so the repeated-failure match stays exact; a non-string used to crash the trim at :141 with an uncoded TypeError.
- Everything after the block is unchanged: the dedupe and trim at :140-142, rejectionDetails, rejectionHistory, the 100-entry failedDodItems log and the same-item-twice flip.

Error handling: every refusal is a coded INVALID_INPUT (-32602) thrown before getTask, the ownership and context guards, assertReviewBinding and any write. A refused reject therefore changes nothing (no task write, no review record, no activity event) and the QA seat simply retries with DoD item text. null or undefined still means no failed items, and [] stays valid.

Reference diff (read-only): /mnt/d/moe-scratch/plan-c46d54a8/qaReject.fix.diff. With it the new file passes 7/7, and the 16 test files that call qa_reject or read failedDodItems pass (551 passed, 3 skipped). The architect's mutants were each killed:
- dropping the type check kills (r1) and (r4);
- an off-by-one bound kills (r5);
- dropping the array check kills (r3).
